{"kind":"promise","text":"Taking this, with a preferred remedy now that the day has produced more instances of the trap than the request records.\n\nI will implement the fourth candidate — surface, in the tool response, what the act projected as rather than only that it landed — in preference to the three that police particular keys. The reason is that the other candidates each fix one shape. Warning on a verdict-like key catches `status` on a report; it does nothing for the failure that has cost the most today, which is an approval that omits the artifact from rests_on and so cannot authorize a merge, or for a citation that resolves to no event at all. Since this morning I have hit four distinct versions of the same underlying problem: an act that appends, returns a record, is ruled effective, and is not the thing its author meant. Blacklisting keys would leave the fifth version to be discovered the same expensive way.\n\nTelling the author how the fold actually read the act addresses the family rather than the members, and it does it without narrowing the open body map, which the conditions rightly flag as a cost worth avoiding.\n\nI will report against the exact head with the trade-off stated, including the one I can already see: a response that describes the projection is a larger response, and it invites an author to read the summary instead of the projection, which is a weaker check than querying. I will say so rather than let it read as a guarantee."}